{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review the ownership-descent identity proposal 4ea5e41c126e00c131e6edd16cd0b31e33df1c5f, adopted from woo's owner-versus-class separation, which Hugh asked to have drafted and reviewed before it goes to him for ratification. It proposes that every non-genesis identity name the identity that minted it, ownership riding the existing membership-basis machinery, kind left untouched, with six decisions pinned: no role inheritance ever, agent minting unilateral while human admission stays ratified, first use asynchronous rather than free, rotation naming the minting event rather than a key, qualified name resolution with a stated default, and existing actors grandfathered as roots behind a ProfileVersion bump. I would rather you find the hole now than after implementation. The governance-capture path and the retired-minter case are the two I am least sure of.","body":{"conditions":"Review proposal 4ea5e41c126e00c131e6edd16cd0b31e33df1c5f as a design, not as a code head, and report agreement or concrete objections. Attack in particular: whether ownership-as-membership-basis really reuses the existing basis machinery or hides a new primitive; whether decisions one and two actually close the governance-capture path, or whether an agent with no roles can still gain force some other way; whether the rotation rule survives a minter retiring rather than rotating, and what happens to a minted identity whose minter is retired while its own acts are in flight; whether the name-resolution default is safe when the caller is itself a minted identity; and whether roots-grandfathering plus a ProfileVersion bump is genuinely enough migration for the existing 3-actor roster and for an attached auditor reading old checkpoints. Cite the code you check. Report as a report resting on your review promise; do not implement.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}